WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.207
"How to Get Enough “Lifespan Essential” Polyphenols"

00:00:07.332 --> 00:00:09.320
Polyphenols are
among the front runners

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.800
in developing dietary approaches
to fight age-associated disease.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.640
There is such
a critical mass of data

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.880
in favor of the protective
benefits of these

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.640
"lifespan essentials"

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.960
that recommended daily
intakes of polyphenols

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.400
have been proposed.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.680
More than 8,000
different polyphenols

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.680
have been identified,

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.160
though only a small proportion

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.680
have had their health effects
cataloged.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.960
Shared structural features
make them among

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.080
the most powerful dietary
antioxidants in vitro,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.600
but in our bodies,

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.560
mechanistic effects have been
attributed to their ability

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:45.880
to modulate cellular signal
transduction pathways.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.640
In the PREDIMED trial,

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.280
those in the top fifth
of polyphenol intake

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.600
had a 37 percent reduction
in all-cause mortality

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.200
compared to those in
the lower fifth of consumption,

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.400
which over an adult lifetime
could translate into

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.920
about four more years of life.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.240
However, the high polyphenol
group was eating

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.360
nine servings of fruits
and vegetables a day

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.280
compared to the five a day eaten
by the low polyphenol group;

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.960
so, it's possible there were
other phytonutrients at play.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.240
Coffee and tea are major
sources, but in terms of foods,

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.920
polyphenols come primarily from
vegetables, fruits, then beans.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.600
There are about a half dozen
different types of polyphenols,

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.600
including phenolic acids
like the curcumin in turmeric

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.280
or the lignans
concentrated in flaxseeds,

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.040
but the largest class
are the flavonoids.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.240
Plant eaters may consume

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.760
more than 4,000 different
types of flavonoids

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:39.440
on a daily basis.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.560
Flavonoids include the
anthocyanin pigments in berries,

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.360
the isoflavones in soy foods,

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.640
the catechins in tea like EGCG,

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:47.960
the flavonones in citrus,

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.320
and the flavonols,
like quercetin and fisetin,

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.200
which people get from foods
like apples, onions,

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.160
and strawberries.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.840
Like polyphenols generally,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.480
those who consume
the most flavonoids

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.400
tend to live
significantly longer,

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.280
according to a meta-analysis
of 16 cohort studies

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.160
following nearly
a half million people

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.440
for 5 to 28 years.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.960
However, those who get more

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.000
are also more likely
to be college-educated,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.920
non-Hispanic white
with a higher income.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.880
The sharpest
socioeconomic gradient

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.040
was found for berry flavonoids.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.200
If you look at
individual foods, though,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.480
as Harvard researchers did
in the Nurses' Health Study II,

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.240
it wasn't just
blueberries and strawberries

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.840
that were correlated with a
lower risk of premature death,

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:31.480
but also
more affordable produce,

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:34.160
such as apples and bell peppers.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.200
To prove cause and effect,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.160
researchers randomized
middle-aged men and women

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.880
to eat higher-
or lower-polyphenol diets

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.280
in the Polyphenol
Intervention Trial.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:44.200
Eight weeks later,

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.000
the higher-polyphenol diet group

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.760
enjoyed significant improvements
in quality of life—

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:50.320
both physically and mentally—

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.520
compared to
the lower-polyphenol diet group.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.720
But what did the two diets
consist of?

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.160
The lower-polyphenol diet group

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.080
was told to restrict
fruits and vegetables

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.480
to no more than
2 servings a day,

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.400
whereas the higher-polyphenol
group was to eat six servings

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.080
(including a portion
of berries),

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.880
plus two ounces (60 g)
of dark chocolate a day.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.520
So, it could have been any
number of fruit and vegetable

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.080
components besides polyphenols
responsible for the benefit.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:17.120
Just the enforced
chocolate consumption

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.240
might have boosted well-being!

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.080
A more rigorous study design

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:22.600
might be
to randomize both groups

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:25.680
to the same number
of high-versus-low-polyphenol

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.280
fruit and vegetable servings.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:29.880
In the Nurses' Health Study,

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.520
you may have noticed that
one food source of flavonoids

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:37.280
was associated with an increased
risk of all-cause mortality:

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:38.680
grapefruit.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.320
Frequent grapefruit consumers

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.160
were more likely
to die prematurely

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.800
than their non-grapefruit-eating
compatriots

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.160
matched for a large number

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.720
of demographic
and lifestyle factors.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:52.080
This was partly chalked up
to grapefruit's suppression

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.520
of a set of detoxification
enzymes in our intestines

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.880
that may metabolize half
the common drugs on the market.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.720
Broccoli has
the opposite effect,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.720
boosting detox enzymes

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.720
such that some drugs
can be cleared faster,

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:07.800
whereas even a single cup
(240 ml) of grapefruit juice

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.680
can reduce the rate
of drug clearance for days,

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.880
increasing the risk
of potentially life-threatening

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:15.720
drug side effects.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.240
That's why grapefruit
has been called

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.640
a "pharmacologist's nightmare."

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:20.920
If you're on medications

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:23.920
(including many common heart
and blood pressure pills)

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:25.840
make sure to talk
to your prescriber

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.600
about your grapefruit habits.

